            <div xml:id="d555">

                <pb facs="0639" n="381" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg381"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 441.11B74/13</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Great
                        Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-05-06">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 6, 1915</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1512. Your 2002, April 28. Replying communication <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Segurança</hi> inform Foreign Office owners advise Department that,
                    although British Consul General supervised loading vessel, he did not inform
                    them of practice of consigning shipments to Netherlands Oversea Trust. If
                    British Government wish this practice followed as a matter of convenience, they
                    should bring it to attention of shippers.</p>
                <p>The United States Government does not object to consignment of American shipments
                    to Netherlands Oversea Trust, if shippers voluntarily adopt that plan, but it
                    does object to detention by British Government of non-contraband shipments until
                    reconsigned to Netherlands Oversea Trust. Shipments consigned to other
                    consignees in Holland have same legal status as those consigned to Netherlands
                    Oversea Trust. There is no legal justification for detention non-contraband
                    shipments, and burden of proof is on British Government to establish contraband
                    character, and not on shipper to establish non-contraband character.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bryan</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
